(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatology-ii-quiz"]={"title":"Dermatology II Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient with chronic urticaria is still symptomatic on a standard dose of a non-sedating antihistamine. What comes next?","choices":["Increase to up to four times the standard dose, and consider adding an H2 blocker","Switch immediately to a long-term oral corticosteroid taken daily","Switch across to a first-generation sedating antihistamine at standard dose","Stop the antihistamine and start a leukotriene antagonist alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — omalizumab and ciclosporin follow if that fails. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which features separate a fixed drug eruption from erythema multiforme?","choices":["It produces wheals that migrate, blanch fully and resolve within twenty-four hours of first appearing","It produces extensive epidermal detachment with mucosal involvement and systemic toxicity","It produces tender nodules on the anterior shins that never go on to ulcerate","It recurs at the same site, leaves residual hyperpigmentation, and has few lesions without three concentric zones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the three-zone target is what defines an erythema multiforme lesion. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which drug classes are classic causes of phototoxicity?","choices":["Aromatic anticonvulsants, allopurinol and nevirapine","Oral contraceptives, sulfonamides and bromides","Tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, amiodarone and thiazides","Sunscreen chemicals, sulfonamides and phenothiazines"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside furosemide, voriconazole, some nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and psoralens. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What is the main differential for localised granuloma annulare, and how is it separated?","choices":["Rheumatoid nodules, which are firm, periarticular and serology positive","Lichen planus, which is violaceous and polygonal with Wickham striae","Tinea corporis, which has a scaling border and is potassium hydroxide positive","Necrobiosis lipoidica, which has a yellow-waxy atrophic centre on the shins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — granuloma annulare has no scale on its ring. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How is a positive photopatch test interpreted?","choices":["Any reaction indicates phototoxicity regardless of which patch responds","A reaction only on the irradiated patch indicates photoallergy; a reaction on both indicates contact allergy","A reaction on both patches indicates photoallergy; a reaction on one indicates phototoxicity","A reaction on the non-irradiated patch alone indicates photoallergy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the suspected photohaptens are applied in duplicate and one set irradiated with ultraviolet A. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"What is beremagene geperpavec?","choices":["A non-adherent biological dressing designed for fragile skin","A topical gene therapy gel approved in 2023 for dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa","A monoclonal antibody given intravenously for junctional epidermolysis bullosa","An oral small molecule that restores keratin 5 and 14 function"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, accessible through dermatology referral centres and gene therapy trials. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which systemic complications are named in toxic epidermal necrolysis?","choices":["Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, acute rheumatic fever and a post-infectious reactive arthritis of the knee","Neurocutaneous melanosis, hydrocephalus and seizures in early childhood","Symblepharon alone, with no involvement of any other organ system","Sepsis, acute respiratory distress syndrome, acute kidney injury, electrolyte imbalance, hypovolaemia and gastrointestinal haemorrhage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — survivors also face sicca syndrome, lung disease and psychological trauma. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What is the role of sub-antimicrobial dose doxycycline in rosacea?","choices":["It reduces flushing by blocking alpha-adrenergic receptors in the skin","It prevents rhinophyma from developing in male patients","It is approved for papulopustular rosacea and works as an anti-inflammatory rather than an antimicrobial","It eradicates Demodex folliculorum from the follicles over the course of several weeks of therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, at 40 mg modified-release daily. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A patient has had four episodes of herpes-associated erythema multiforme this year. Which chronic measure is appropriate?","choices":["Annual phototherapy given as a five-week course each spring","Suppressive antiviral therapy, such as aciclovir 400 mg twice daily","A standing prescription for oral prednisone to take at each episode","Long-term oral antihistamines taken continuously through the year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — preventing the herpes recurrence prevents the eruption. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What causes phytophotodermatitis, and how does it present?","choices":["Furanocoumarins in plants such as lime, celery and parsley plus ultraviolet A, producing linear streaked hyperpigmentation","Bergapten in bergamot oil used in fragrances and cosmetics, producing drip-pattern hyperpigmentation of the neck","A persistent eczematous eruption in chronically exposed skin of older males","A delayed hypersensitivity response to an ultraviolet-induced photoantigen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — classically after lime juice and sun exposure, with painful blistering acutely. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which investigation is the gold standard for epidermolysis bullosa?","choices":["Transmission electron microscopy with immunofluorescence antigen mapping","Perilesional direct immunofluorescence looking for granular immunoglobulin A","Punch biopsy showing full-thickness epidermal necrosis of the specimen","Serum enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay for anti-desmoglein antibodies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a genetic panel and nutritional labs support it. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Which single intervention most improves survival in Stevens-Johnson syndrome?","choices":["Immediate withdrawal of the causative drug, since each day of delay worsens prognosis","Early administration of intravenous immunoglobulin at 1 g/kg daily for three days","Prophylactic broad-spectrum antibiotics started on admission","High-dose systemic corticosteroids given throughout the admission"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — earlier withdrawal is strongly associated with improved survival. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Which biologic is approved for refractory chronic urticaria?","choices":["Omalizumab, an anti-immunoglobulin E antibody given every four weeks","Infliximab, a tumour necrosis factor alpha inhibitor given intravenously","Dupilumab, an interleukin-4 receptor antagonist given subcutaneously","Etanercept, a tumour necrosis factor inhibitor given subcutaneously"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, at 300 mg subcutaneously. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which topical agent is the only one approved for photoaging, and how does it work?","choices":["Niacinamide, which reduces inflammation and improves skin sallowness","Tretinoin, which stimulates collagen synthesis and inhibits matrix metalloproteinase activity","Hydroquinone, which inhibits melanin production within the epidermis","Vitamin C, which neutralises reactive oxygen species and stimulates new collagen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, requiring a minimum of six to twelve months for visible results. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Which pyoderma gangrenosum variant is most often associated with haematologic malignancy?","choices":["The bullous variant, with tense superficial bullae and blue-grey overhanging edges on the upper limbs and face","The classic ulcerative variant, with an undermined violaceous border on the lower extremities only","The pustular variant, with painful discrete pustules arising on inflamed skin","The vegetative variant, with a slowly enlarging superficial verrucous plaque"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — acute myeloid leukaemia is the named association. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is first-line treatment for acute urticaria?","choices":["Intramuscular epinephrine at 0.3 mg given immediately on presentation","Omalizumab 300 mg subcutaneously repeated every four weeks","A first-generation sedating antihistamine taken four times daily","A second-generation antihistamine such as cetirizine, loratadine or fexofenadine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a short prednisone course is added for severe acute disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is used for suppressive therapy in recurrent herpes-associated erythema multiforme?","choices":["Hydroxychloroquine 200 mg twice daily taken indefinitely","Prednisone 40 to 60 mg daily for five days at each recurrence","Acyclovir 400 mg twice daily or valacyclovir 500 mg daily","Dapsone 25 to 200 mg daily with regular monitoring of the blood count"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dapsone or hydroxychloroquine are used for refractory non-herpes recurrent disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which group carries markedly increased actinic keratosis risk?","choices":["Patients with coeliac disease, at roughly 30 times the risk","Organ transplant recipients, at roughly 65 times the risk","Patients with atopic dermatitis, at roughly 20 times the risk","Patients with inflammatory bowel disease, at roughly 10 times the risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside Fitzpatrick types I and II, male sex, age over fifty and outdoor occupation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"Which features suggest malignant rather than benign acanthosis nigricans?","choices":["Gradual onset over years confined to the posterior neck and axillae","Velvety hyperpigmented plaques appearing only in the inframammary folds","Lesions that wipe away completely with an isopropyl alcohol swab","Rapid onset, extensive involvement, oral mucosal change and tripe palms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — malignant disease is paraneoplastic, especially with gastric adenocarcinoma. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which features in an actinic keratosis are described as concerning?","choices":["A sandpaper texture on palpation of the lesion","A diameter between two and ten millimetres","A skin-coloured to red-brown appearance overall","Pruritus, tenderness and bleeding"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a hypertrophic, thickened and verrucous lesion also carries higher malignant potential. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"Which single feature most usefully distinguishes erythema nodosum from nodular vasculitis?","choices":["Erythema nodosum nodules are painless on palpation","Erythema nodosum nodules occur exclusively in men","Erythema nodosum nodules do not ulcerate","Erythema nodosum nodules appear only on the posterior legs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and they sit on the anterior shins whereas nodular vasculitis favours the posterior legs and ulcerates. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about patients with Fitzpatrick types IV to VI?","choices":["They are not immune to ultraviolet damage or skin cancer, and melanoma is frequently diagnosed at advanced stages","They carry no meaningful skin cancer risk and need no routine photoprotection","They burn more readily than types I to III but tan more deeply afterwards","They should use SPF 15 rather than SPF 30, on the basis that higher factors confer no additional benefit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — delayed diagnosis is common because of a lower index of suspicion. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which advice is given about blisters in second-degree sunburn?","choices":["Apply a high-potency topical corticosteroid directly to them","Cover them with silver sulfadiazine to prevent colonisation","Do not pop them, because intact blisters are protective","Drain them under sterile conditions to reduce infection risk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with cool compresses and early nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for pain. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"What is the cornerstone of long-term management in dermatitis herpetiformis?","choices":["Sulfapyridine taken indefinitely in place of dietary change","A strict lifelong gluten-free diet","Indefinite daily dapsone at the lowest effective dose","Long-term high-potency topical corticosteroids to affected sites"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it may allow dapsone to be reduced or stopped over one to two years. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which features should raise suspicion of secondary rather than primary hyperhidrosis?","choices":["Sweating confined to the axillae with a family history of the same","Generalised sweating, nocturnal sweating, or an asymmetric distribution","Bilateral palmar sweating that has been present since adolescence","Sweating that worsens with anxiety and improves in a cool room"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — primary disease is bilateral, focal and absent during sleep. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What kind of process is erythema nodosum, histologically?","choices":["A lobular panniculitis with associated vasculitis","A palisading granuloma around degenerating collagen","A dense neutrophilic infiltrate without organisms","A septal panniculitis without vasculitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the most common form of panniculitis, a delayed hypersensitivity reaction in subcutaneous fat. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which referrals are named for erythema nodosum, and what determines each?","choices":["Pulmonology or rheumatology for Löfgren syndrome, gastroenterology for inflammatory bowel disease, infectious diseases for tuberculosis or endemic mycoses, dermatology for atypical cases","Dermatology in every single case, since the diagnosis requires a deep incisional biopsy taken from one of the affected nodules before any treatment at all begins","Vascular surgery in every case, since the nodules reflect venous disease","Oncology in every case, since the condition is usually paraneoplastic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the referral follows the suspected underlying cause. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which haplotypes are strongly associated with dermatitis herpetiformis?","choices":["HLA-Cw6 and HLA-B27","HLA-A31 and HLA-BW35","HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8","HLA-B*15:02 and HLA-B*58:01"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and nearly all patients have underlying gluten-sensitive enteropathy even when gastrointestinally asymptomatic. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the hardening phenomenon in polymorphous light eruption?","choices":["Loss of pigment in the affected areas after each episode","Improvement through the summer as tolerance develops after repeated exposure","Thickening and hardening of the skin from repeated scratching of the eruption","Progressive scarring of affected sites over successive seasons"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is also why prophylactic narrowband ultraviolet B in spring is the most effective preventive strategy. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which biologic is approved for pyoderma gangrenosum in inflammatory bowel disease?","choices":["Omalizumab at 300 mg subcutaneously every four weeks","Etanercept at 25 to 50 mg subcutaneously","Dupilumab given subcutaneously every two weeks","Infliximab at 5 mg/kg intravenously"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the biologic of choice in that setting, with adalimumab and ustekinumab for refractory cases. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 60"}]};
